 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "KELVINGROVE ART GALLERY. Built of red sandstone for the 1901 International Exhibition, and now one of the most visited museums in Britain. It has a Spitfire hanging from the ceiling, an elephant called Sophie, a Dal\u00ed, and an organ that gives a free concert most days. Entry is free. Ask for the elephant."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "GEORGE SQUARE. The front lawn of the City Chambers, lined with statues of writers, scientists and soldiers, including Walter Scott up on a very tall column. The square has also hosted film crews, Christmas markets and one truly ambitious pigeon."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "THE DUKE AND HIS CONE. Glasgow's famous Duke of Wellington statue always seems to wear a traffic cone. The council has removed it many times. The city has put it back many times more. Scotland's local politics, in one object."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "SQUINTY BRIDGE. Officially the Clyde Arc, opened in 2006. The arch leans to one side and the deck curves, so everyone calls it the Squinty Bridge. Nobody has ever called it the Clyde Arc without a wee smile."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "THE CLYDE. The river that built the city. Once the shipyards on its banks launched a big share of the world's ships. Today its quays are for walking, cycling and watching the water change colour."
  },
  {
   "x": 51,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "THE FINNIESTON CRANE. A giant cantilever crane from 1931, built to lift locomotives and boilers onto ships. Glasgow-built engines were sent all over the world. The crane is a monument now, and it still looks like it could lift your house."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "GLASCHU. Glasgow comes from an old name usually translated as 'the dear green place', and there are more parks here than you'd expect. St Mungo, the founder, left a rhyme: a bird that never flew, a tree that never grew, a bell that never rang, a fish that never swam. They're on the city's coat of arms."
  }
 ],
